# Docker build context excludes (repo root is the context).
#
# Two consumers with different stakes. Docker/docker-compose.yml bind-mounts the repo and
# COPYs nothing, so for it this is only about not shipping gigabytes to the daemon.
# Docker/Dockerfile.runtime does `COPY . /src`, so for that one every path listed here is
# a path that would otherwise land in a published image's builder layer. Anything private,
# large, or generated belongs below.
#
# Pattern note: a pattern without `**` matches at the context root only, so `.vs/` and
# `.venv/` need the `**/` prefix to catch nested copies.

# Build outputs and caches
out/
build/
# MilaPy's POST_BUILD stages the compiled extension INTO the source tree, so a developer's
# checkout accumulates one per interpreter and platform it has ever built (untracked --
# only __init__.py is in git). The builder stage compiles its own against the container's
# interpreter, so shipping the host's copies would put stale Linux extensions and Windows
# .pyd files in a Linux image.
**/_mila*.so
**/_mila*.pyd
**/.vs/
.ccache/
**/.venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.egg-info/
**/*.pyc
TestResults/

# Version control and CI metadata
.git/
.github/

# Private, gitignored trees. Dev/ is not part of Mila (~700 MB of local scratch) and
# .internal/ holds unpublished marketing copy — neither is source, and a COPY that
# swept them into a builder layer would be shipping them.
Dev/
.internal/

# Large data — model weights and datasets are converted offline and bind-mounted, never
# baked in. No .bin file is tracked, so the recursive exclude costs the build nothing.
Data/Models/
Data/Datasets/
**/*.bin

# Editor / tooling / OS noise
.devcontainer/
.claude/
